
  <sn><md>### Soil Alkalization

**Definition:**
Soil alkalization is the process by which soil becomes alkaline (high pH) due to the accumulation of **sodium carbonate (Na₂CO₃)** and **sodium bicarbonate (NaHCO₃)**, raising soil pH above **8.5**. It is also called **sodification** when excess Na⁺ occupies exchange sites.

**Causes:**
- **High evaporation** in arid and semi-arid regions, leaving salts behind
- **Irrigation with sodic water** containing high Na⁺
- **Poor drainage** causing waterlogging and salt accumulation
- **Over-irrigation** without proper leaching
- **Weathering of parent rocks** rich in sodium

**Process:**
$$
\text{Na}_2\text{CO}_3 + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow 2\text{Na}^+ + 2\text{OH}^- + \text{CO}_2
$$
The released OH⁻ ions increase soil pH. Na⁺ displaces Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ on exchange sites, making soil **sodic**.

**Effects on Soil:**
- **High pH (&gt;8.5)** — nutrients like Fe, Mn, Zn, P become unavailable
- **Poor soil structure** — Na⁺ disperses clay particles, destroying aggregates
- **Reduced infiltration** — soil becomes impermeable to water and air
- **Crust formation** — hard surface layer prevents seedling emergence
- **Toxic effects** — excess Na⁺ and carbonate harm plant roots

**Effects on Crops:**
- Stunted growth and yellowing (chlorosis)
- Poor root development
- Reduced water and nutrient uptake
- Low crop yield

**Remediation:**
- **Gypsum (CaSO₄)** application:
$$
\text{Soil} - 2\text{Na}^+ + \text{CaSO}_4 \rightarrow \text{Soil} - \text{Ca}^{2+} + \text{Na}_2\text{SO}_4
$$
- **Leaching** with good quality water to remove displaced Na⁺
- **Organic matter** addition to improve structure
- **Proper drainage** to prevent salt accumulation
- **Growing salt-tolerant crops** (barley, sugar beet)

**Example:**
In parts of **Punjab and Haryana (India)**, excessive canal irrigation without drainage has caused soil alkalization, reducing wheat and rice yields. Gypsum treatment and leaching have helped reclaim these soils.

**Conclusion:**
Soil alkalization is the accumulation of sodium salts raising pH above 8.5, degrading soil structure and reducing crop growth. It is managed by **gypsum application, leaching, drainage, and organic matter addition**.</md></sn>
